thread:
jamie: Heads-up: ran into weird fetch behavior in our JS SDK when used in CJS Node apps + Vercel/Next Edge functions. Node 18+ global fetch vs undici duplication causing stream/type oddities. Anyone hit this?

lee: Yep — saw this in a customer repro. It usually shows up when bundlers polyfill fetch for the browser target. Do you have a minimal repro?

jamie: Minimal repro: our package ships as ESM and we have a CJS fallback that does `require('undici').fetch`. In Next Edge, `globalThis.fetch` exists but behaves like the edge/web fetch (no Node ReadableStream methods). Code paths expecting node-stream-like semantics call `res.body.getReader()` and blow up. Also TS types conflict (lib.dom vs node).

mira: @jamie can you paste the detection snippet you used? my guess is dynamic resolution is the root cause.

jamie: ```js
// simplified detection we shipped
const getFetch = () => {
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function') return globalThis.fetch;
  try {
    return require('undici').fetch; // breaks in pure ESM or when bundlers rewrite require
  } catch {
    throw new Error('No fetch available - please polyfill or install undici');
  }
};
```

lee: Two separate issues: 1) ESM vs CJS resolution (require can't be used from ESM bundles), and 2) shipping/duplicating fetchs causes AbortSignal/request body identity problems and streaming incompatibilities. Requiring undici inside a consumer that already has a global fetch can still lead to two different implementations (e.g. different Request/Response constructors).

jamie: Also hit auth/retry gotchas: our auto-retry wrapper retries on 502 for POSTs and re-sends non-idempotent payloads. Oops. Customers hit duplicate-side effects.

mira: Proposal: publish a tiny runtime shim with three exported helpers: `getFetch()`, `isWebStream(res)`, `wrapRetry(opts)` and default `getFetch` to `globalThis.fetch`. Document that SDK will only auto-retry idempotent methods by default and expose a retry hook for consumers to opt-in for POSTs.

lee: For ESM consumers we can use dynamic import: `const undici = await import('undici'); const f = undici.fetch;` For CJS consumers we can keep the generated CJS entry that does `module.exports = require('./cjs-entry')` which itself does a lazy require. Important: do NOT bundle undici into the published package; add it as an optional peerDependency or runtime dependency so we don't produce duplicate global instances.

infra-bot: :information_source: infra note: Next/Edge runtime change (Next v14) rolling tomorrow — expect stricter web-stream behavior. Test your SDK against the new runtime to avoid regressions.

lee: Types: recommend adding a tiny types shim to the package, e.g. `types.d.ts` with:
```ts
declare module 'undici' {
  export function fetch(input: RequestInfo, init?: RequestInit): Promise<Response>;
}
```
and doc guidance for customers to set `tsconfig.lib = ["DOM","ES2022"]` and `skipLibCheck: true` if they have `@types/node` that conflicts with DOM types. Also call out that `Response.body` streams differ between node and edge.

jamie: Action items I will take: 1) add `getFetch` helper to SDK and make it the canonical place for runtime fetch detection, 2) change retry policy to only auto-retry idempotent methods, 3) add CI matrix (Node 18/20, Cloudflare Workers, Next Edge) and a small sample ESM/CJS usage doc, 4) include a short migration note for Dedicated customers.

mira: Thanks — please add code snippets for ESM/CJS usage and a short paragraph about bundlers (webpack/rollup/next) and how they might polyfill fetch; include a `package.json` recommendation (export ESM + CJS thin wrapper). Also add a note to the SDK changelog about the retry behavior change. :thumbsup:

lee: One last tip: avoid touching global constructors. When you must interop, convert to plain bodies (ArrayBuffer/ReadableStream) before passing between implementations to reduce two-instance breakage.

jamie: Will do — opening PR with a docs draft + shim implementation now. If anyone wants to pair on the CI matrix tests ping me.
